PlayStation is leaping into the world of  prosumer controllers with the Dual sense  Edge we have the opportunity to check  out the new Gamepad and it feels like a  well-rounded hobbyist controller that's  easy to customize on the Fly the Dual  sense Edge feels and looks nearly  identical to the Dual sense controller  which the lead product manager assured  me was an intentional decision so as to  not alienate players who have already  spent hundreds of hours with the  standard dual sense the customizable  back buttons are the thing that felt the  most different unlike the Xbox Elite  Series 2 controllers 4 there are only  two back buttons on this controller the  paddles jut out slightly more than the  elite controllers meaning it requires a  tap to press rather than the squeeze  motion you would normally do with an  elite controller the package also comes  with an alternative set of back buttons  shaped like half Moons these felt more  natural in our hands as they leaned  right up against our middle fingers of  course you can also remove all the back  buttons altogether if you'd like there  are limits to what you you can and can't  customize when it comes to button  configurations you cannot program  multiple inputs to a single button press  and there are some restrictions with the  touchpad though we didn't get a good  idea of those during the preview the  most useful additions to the Dual sense  Edge are the two function buttons  located just below the analog sticks  holding one down brings up a menu that  allows you to quickly switch controller  profiles adjust headset volume and pop  into a menu to tweak your controller  settings the function buttons feel like  a natural extension of the Dual sense  that makes it easier to access key  features you're likely to need in a  gaming session their location makes them  hard to miss click during heated moments  yet they are easy to access mid-game the  biggest barrier for us when it comes to  controller customization has always been  finding the time to really fine tune it  to fit our needs but the Dual sense Edge  makes it a breeze to find and test  different controller settings the Sleek  new controller menu is also effortless  to navigate once you've created a  profile you can customize button  assignments stick and dead zone  sensitivities trigger dead zones  vibration intensity and Trigger effect  intensity if you flip on the trigger  stops it will automatically adjust  trigger effect sensitivity from the  stick sensitivity Dead Zone menu you can  pick from a handful of default  sensitivity curves for sticks or you can  tweak the sensitivity and dead zone  manually there's a handy visualizer in  this menu as well that shows how much  force you're putting on the sticks with  your preferred settings along with  swapping out the stick caps the Dual  sense Edge also allows you to remove the  stick modules you can pop off the hood  with the little tab on the back of the  controller from there you can unlock the  modules and push them out historically  the sticks on controllers are usually  the first to go so this Edition is a  welcome one the modules run from twenty  dollars on Sony's official storefront  Which is far more reasonable than  purchasing a new controller outright  popping the hood off was a bit of a pain  but ideally you shouldn't have to do  that often annoyingly the hood itself is  made out of a glossy plastic that  attracts smudges and thumb prints in the  few hours we had with the controller we  found it impossible to keep it clean  this certainly isn't a deal breaker but  it will surely be enough to bother some  users hopefully because the hood can be  removed entirely we will see some  aftermarket hoods with a matte finish  while the Dual sense Edge probably won't  cater to Casual PlayStation 5 owners  it's a great piece of kit for those who  take their gaming seriously during our  time with the controller we tested out  God of War Ragnarok Call of Duty Modern  Warfare 2 and Gran Turismo 7. between  the three we found the Dual signs Edge  best suited for modern warfare's Twitchy  gameplay being able to map jump and  slide to the back buttons meant we  didn't have to take our Thumb off the  right analog stick while performing some  of the game's trickier Maneuvers  meanwhile the controller didn't feel as  useful for Gran Turismo 7 and God of War  Ragnarok it's nice being able to map  things to the back buttons but the  appeal of the Dual sense Edge isn't  quite as strong for single player games  where inputs are fewer and the demands  of the player are much less than in a  competitive multiplayer experience like  the elite the controller along with the  charger swappable stick cabs back  buttons and a cord lock are all packaged  in a white hard shell carrying case with  a hole in the back for easy charging  overall the Dual sense Edge feels like a  nice option for PlayStation owners that  might have been looking at Xbox's  offerings with Pro Controller Envy for  two hundred dollars you'll get a  controller that has a familiar form  factor improves ergonomics some new  feature options and a good level of  customization  [Music]  thank you  [Music]
 

